-- title:
--   Surjective product map and rank identity for the Raynaud quotient
-- statement:
--   **Arithmetic and geometric frame.** Fix a prime $p$ and a nonzero modulus $M$ with $p \mid M$ (`hpM`) and $p^2 \nmid M$ (`hpM2`), a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb Z/M)^\times$, and assume (`hHp`) that every unit of $\mathbb Z/M$ whose image under `ZMod.unitsMap` along $M/p \mid M$ is $1$ lies in $H$, i.e. $H$ contains the kernel of $(\mathbb Z/M)^\times \to (\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$; $M/p$ is nonzero. Fix a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $p$ in the sense of `LiesOverPrime` (the image of $p$ is a non-unit of $Pl$), whose residue field is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$. The hypothesis `hj` asserts that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ belongs to `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`, which is the input needed to form the two-chart integral models `toBase p Γ hj` over $R_p =$ `ratLocalizedAt p`.
--
--   Further, $𝔛$ is a model datum `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`, whose fields include properness, flatness, integrality, local finite presentation and normality of the two-chart model at level $\Gamma_M$, properness and relative smoothness of dimension $1$ of the model at level $\Gamma_N$, a curve model over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the geometric function field of $X_H$ together with an isomorphism onto the generic fibre, Galois compatibility of that identification and the pinning of $q$-expansions. Next, $\Lambda$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData` for $p, M, H, Pl$: a section $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ of the base $\operatorname{Spec} R_p$ through $\operatorname{Spec} Pl$ compatible with the generic point, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ with structure morphism $\Lambda.f$, a relative group law $\Lambda.L$ on it, and bijections of the degree-zero class group at level $(M/p)$ and of the class group of the residue-field function field with the groups of sections over the generic and the residual point. Finally $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP` over these data: a scheme $O.G$ with structure morphism $O.g$, a commutative relative group law $O.L$, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ between $J_H =$ `JH M H` $= \mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb Q},$ `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` $)$ and the sections over the generic point, which is additive and Galois-equivariant, the geometric conditions (smooth, separated, locally of finite type, quasi-compact, surjective, with preconnected fibres), Hecke endomorphisms compatible with the group law and with `genOpH`, flatness and surjectivity of multiplication by $n>0$, an integer $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, and the remaining fields of that structure. For $m \in \mathbb N$, `O.finPts m` is the subgroup of $J_H$ generated by the $m$-torsion classes $x$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ for which $O.\mathrm{pts}\,x$ satisfies the extension condition `ExtendsToPlace Pl Λ.σA`, and `O.toricPts m` is the subgroup generated by the range of $O.\mathrm{toricPoint}\,m$ (and is trivial for $m = 0$).
--
--   **Representability hypotheses.** `hrep` asserts that the triple consisting of $O.G$, $O.g$ and the unit section of $O.L$ over the identity, viewed as a `RelativePic0Designation` over $R_p$ for the $\Gamma_M$-level model, represents the subfunctor of the relative Picard functor rigidified along $𝔛.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ cut out by `algEquivZeroCut` (the fibrewise algebraically-trivial condition): there is a Poincaré rigidified line bundle satisfying that condition, it is universal among such bundles, and its pullback along the zero section is trivial. `hrepΛ` asserts the same for $\Lambda.X$, $\Lambda.f$ and the unit section of $\Lambda.L$ relative to the $\Gamma_N$-level model, with rigidification the section obtained from $𝔛.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ by composing with $𝔛.\pi$.
--
--   **The base ring $R^h$.** $R^h$ is a commutative domain which is a Henselian local ring and a discrete valuation ring, equipped with an injective algebra structure over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ whose image lies in $Pl$ (`hRA`), such that $x$ lies in the maximal ideal of $R^h$ exactly when its image has $Pl$-valuation $< 1$ (`hRloc`), together with an algebra structure over $\mathbb Z/p$ for which $x \mapsto 0$ exactly when that same valuation is $< 1$ (`hres`). A set $S \subseteq \mathbb N$ and a unit $d$ of $\mathbb Z/M$ are given, with (`hd`) the image of $d$ in $\mathbb Z/(M/p)$ equal to the class of $p$.
--
--   **The $p$-divisible group $\mathcal G$ and the Tate-module comparison.** $\mathcal G$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) over $R^h$ of parameter $p$ and height $h$: a system of finite free commutative and cocommutative Hopf $R^h$-algebras $\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective bialgebra transitions whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals. Given are an additive map $\Delta$ from $\mathcal G$-points over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (the direct limit of the convolution groups of $R^h$-algebra maps $\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$) to $J_H$, and a $\mathbb Z_p$-linear map $e$ of Tate modules. The hypotheses on them: $\Delta$ is injective (`hΔinj`); for every $v$, the image of the level-$v$ points under $\Delta$ is exactly `O.finPts (p ^ v)` (`hΔlev`); $\Delta$ commutes with the actions of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ and of $R^h$-automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ that agree with them pointwise (`hΔgal`); for each set $S$ of naturals and each Hecke generator $g \in$ [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (a $T_\ell$, a $U_q$ or a diamond) there is a family of bialgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of $\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ commuting with the transitions, such that $\Delta$ carries precomposition of points by $\varphi_v$ to the operator `genOpH M H S g` (`hΔhecke`); $e$ is computed componentwise by $\Delta$ (`he`) and is injective (`heinj`); a Tate-module element lies in the range of $e$ precisely when its $n$-th component lies in `O.finPts (p ^ n)` for all $n$ (`herange`); $e$ intertwines the Tate-module Galois action of $\mathcal G$ with `JH.tateGaloisRep` (`hegal`); the range of $e$ is $p$-saturated (`hsat`); the cokernel of $e$ is $\mathbb Z_p$-linearly isomorphic to $\mathbb Z_p^{O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$ (`hcoker`); and every element of `O.toricPts (p ^ v)` is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point (`htor`).
--
--   **The quotient $\mathcal B$.** $\mathcal B$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) over $R^h$ of height $h_B$, and $\psi_v : \mathcal B.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ are $R^h$-bialgebra maps. The height relations are $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$ (`hhB`) and $h_B = 2h'$ (`hhB2`). The hypotheses: $\psi$ commutes with the transitions (`hψt`); for each $v$, a level-$v$ $\mathcal G$-point composed with $\psi_v$ is the trivial $\mathcal B$-point exactly when its $\Delta$-image lies in `O.toricPts (p ^ v)` (`hψker`); every level-$v$ $\mathcal B$-point over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ arises this way from a $\mathcal G$-point (`hψsurj`); if the $\mathcal B$-point obtained from $x$ reduces to the identity, in the sense that $Pl$-valuation of the difference between its value on $a$ and the image of the counit of $a$ is $<1$ for all $a$, then the same holds for $x$ itself (`hψred`); and (`hperiod`) for each $v$, each $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, each $p^v$-torsion class $z$ in $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb Q},$ `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` $)$ and each level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta y = \sigma \cdot z - z$, the image of $y$ under $\psi_v$ reduces to the identity in this valuation sense.
--
--   **Geometric realisation over $R^h$.** Given are a ring map $\rho^h : R_p \to R^h$ compatible with the structure maps into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (`hρh`) and morphisms $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$ with: $\iota_v$ followed by $O.g$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R^h \to \mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec} \rho^h$ (`hιbase`); the induced morphism into the base change of $O.G$ along $\rho^h$ a closed immersion (`hιcl`); $\iota_v$ killed by multiplication by $p^v$ in $O.L$ (`hιp`); on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points, $O.\mathrm{pts}(\Delta(x))$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the algebra map of $x$ followed by $\iota_v$ (`hιpts`); multiplicativity of $\iota_v$ for points with values in any $R^h$-algebra $B$, with respect to the relative group law (`hιmul`); compatibility with the transitions (`hιt`); for each $S$ and each Hecke generator a family $\varphi_v$ commuting with the transitions, compatible through $\iota$ with $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S\,g$, and inducing `genOpH` on $\Delta$-images (`hιhecke`); and (`hιfin`) for each $v$, the morphism $j_v$ induced by $\iota_v$ into the pullback along $\rho^h$ of the $p^v$-kernel of $O.L$ is simultaneously an open and a closed immersion, and every point of that pullback lying over the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} R^h$ lies in the image of $j_v$.
--
--   **The special fibre.** A morphism $\sigma_p : \operatorname{Spec} \mathbb F_p \to \operatorname{Spec} R_p$ is given with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R^h \to \mathbb F_p$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}\rho^h$ equal to $\sigma_p$ (`hσp`); morphisms $\iota^p_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb F_p \otimes_{R^h} \mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G \times_{\,} \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb F_p$ exhibiting $\iota_v$ base-changed along $\sigma_p$, by compatibility with both projections (`hιp₁`, `hιp₂`); and a pair $q_0, q_1$ of morphisms over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb F_p$ from the $\sigma_p$-fibre of $O.g$ to the $\sigma_p$-fibre of $\Lambda.f$. The residue field of $Pl$ is an $\mathbb F_p$-algebra, with (`hfac`) $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathbb F_p \to \operatorname{ResidueField} Pl$ followed by $\sigma_p$ equal to `resPt Pl` followed by $\Lambda.\sigma_A$. Each $q_i$ is a homomorphism for the base-changed group laws (`hqmul`), and (`hqbc`) each $q_i$ agrees, after the base-change maps between the pullbacks over the residue field of $Pl$ and over $\mathbb F_p$, with the component morphism $O.\mathrm{abqFibre}\,i$ recorded in $O$. Moreover $\mathbb F_p \otimes_{R^h} \psi_v$ is injective for every $v$ (`hjB`).
--
--   **The $p$-divisible group $\mathcal A$ over the base ring.** $\mathcal A$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) over `baseRing p` of height $h_\Lambda$, with $h_B = 2 h_\Lambda$ (`hBΛ`), and $\sigma_p$ is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map `baseRing p` $\to \mathbb F_p$ (`hσp'`). Morphisms $\iota'_v : \operatorname{Spec}((\mathcal A_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}\,v) \to \Lambda.X \times \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb F_p$ are given, where $\mathcal A_{\mathbb F_p} = \mathcal A.\mathrm{baseChange}(\mathbb Z/p)$, which are morphisms over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb F_p$ (`hι'base`), closed immersions (`hι'cl`), killed by multiplication by $p^v$ with the induced morphism into the $p^v$-kernel of $\Lambda.L \otimes \mathbb F_p$ an isomorphism (`hι'p`), multiplicative on points with values in any $\mathbb F_p$-algebra (`hι'mul`), and compatible with the transitions of $\mathcal A_{\mathbb F_p}$ (`hι't`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $v \in \mathbb N$, every pair of morphisms $r_i : \operatorname{Spec}((\mathcal B_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}\,v) \to \Lambda.X \times \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb F_p$ ($i \in \{0,1\}$) and every pair of $\mathbb F_p$-bialgebra maps $\rho_i : (\mathcal A_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}\,v \to (\mathcal B_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}\,v$ such that
--
--   (i) for each $i$, $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathbb F_p \otimes_{R^h} \psi_v$ followed by $r_i$ equals $\iota^p_v$ followed by the underlying morphism of $q_i$, and
--
--   (ii) for each $i$, $\operatorname{Spec} \rho_i$ followed by $\iota'_v$ equals $r_i$,
--
--   the following three assertions hold.
--
--   First, the product map `Algebra.TensorProduct.productMap` of $\rho_0$ and $\rho_1$, that is the $\mathbb F_p$-algebra map $(\mathcal A_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}\,v \otimes_{\mathbb F_p} (\mathcal A_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}\,v \to (\mathcal B_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}\,v$ sending $x \otimes y$ to $\rho_0(x)\rho_1(y)$, is surjective.
--
--   Second, the rank identity
--   $$\dim_{\mathbb F_p} (\mathcal B_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}\,v = \bigl(\dim_{\mathbb F_p} (\mathcal A_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}\,v\bigr)^2$$
--   holds, the right-hand side being written in Lean as the product of the two equal factors.
--
--   Third, for every pair $r'_i$ of morphisms $\operatorname{Spec}((\mathcal B_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}(v+1)) \to \Lambda.X \times \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb F_p$ and every pair of $\mathbb F_p$-bialgebra maps $\rho'_i : (\mathcal A_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}(v+1) \to (\mathcal B_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}(v+1)$ satisfying the conditions (i) and (ii) at level $v+1$ (with $\psi_{v+1}$, $\iota^p_{v+1}$ and $\iota'_{v+1}$), and every $i$, the square commutes: the transition of $\mathcal B_{\mathbb F_p}$ at $v$ composed after $\rho'_i$ equals $\rho_i$ composed after the transition of $\mathcal A_{\mathbb F_p}$ at $v$.
--
--   Thus the conclusion records surjectivity of the product map and the equality of ranks, rather than the resulting isomorphism itself, together with the compatibility of the $\rho_i$ with the transition maps.
--
--   This is the algebraic heart of the identification of the special fibre at $p$ (with $p \| M$) of the quotient of the $p$-divisible group attached to the $p$-power `finPts` of $J_H$ by its toric part: surjectivity of the product map and the rank equality say that the pair $(\rho_0,\rho_1)$ presents $(\mathcal B_{\mathbb F_p}).\mathrm{level}\,v$ as a closed subscheme of the square of the level-$v$ torsion of the special fibre of $\Lambda$ of the correct order, and the third clause makes the presentation compatible with the tower. It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_bialgEquiv_baseChange_raynaudQuotient_tensorProduct_levelTorsion_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_bialgEquiv_baseChange_raynaudQuotient_tensorProduct_levelTorsion_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness), which converts these three assertions into a bialgebra isomorphism, in the analysis of the reduction at $p$ of the Jacobian of $X_H$ used for lowering the level at $p$.
